    <string name="help_title">How it works</string>
    <string name="help_what_title">What is a plugin</string>
    <string name="help_what_body">A .zeta file containing compiled Kotlin and everything it needs. ZetaForge loads it at run time and calls it — the app was never built with any knowledge of it.</string>
    <string name="help_install_title">Installing one</string>
    <string name="help_install_body">Press Import and pick a .zeta file, or open one from a file manager. It is copied into ZetaForge’s private storage and verified before anything runs.</string>
    <string name="help_settings_title">Configuring one</string>
    <string name="help_settings_body">Each plugin declares its own settings, and ZetaForge builds the form. Defaults are chosen by whoever wrote the plugin.</string>
    <string name="help_schedule_title">Running it on its own</string>
    <string name="help_schedule_body">Schedule tells ZetaForge when to run a plugin without you: a time, an interval, chosen days, and conditions such as only while charging.</string>
    <string name="help_permissions_title">Permissions</string>
    <string name="help_permissions_body">A plugin declares what it needs and why. ZetaForge asks you at the start of every run, and a refusal stops the run instead of failing halfway.</string>
    <string name="help_trouble_title">If a scheduled run does not happen</string>
    <string name="help_trouble_body">Almost always battery optimisation. Open Diagnostics: it checks everything a background run needs and takes you straight to whatever is missing.</string>
    <string name="help_write_title">Writing your own</string>
    <string name="help_write_body">Plugins are built with the zeta command line tool. See the project documentation.</string>

